# Check how switching from Stripe to Adyen affects your integration

Check whether your GOV.UK Pay integration needs changes before your service switches from Stripe to Adyen.

This guidance is for people responsible for maintaining and testing your service's technical integration with GOV.UK Pay.

You may work for the organisation using GOV.UK Pay or for an external supplier.

It explains the integration differences you need to consider when your service switches from Stripe to Adyen.

Use this guidance to:

* check whether your integration is affected
* identify any changes you need to make
* plan and run tests
* tell GOV.UK Pay if something is missing or could prevent your migration

**Last updated:** 7 October 2026

We will update this guidance when implementation or testing information changes.

GOV.UK Pay will email an admin user for your service if a change could affect work you have already planned or completed.

## When to contact GOV.UK Pay

Email GOV.UK Pay support at <a href="mailto:govuk-pay-support@digital.cabinet-office.gov.uk">govuk-pay-support@digital.cabinet-office.gov.uk</a>  if:

* you identify an integration impact that is not covered here
* you cannot determine whether your integration is affected
* an instruction does not match the behaviour you observe
* you cannot test a change that you need to make
* you need significant development or supplier work
* the work could affect when your service is able to migrate

## What will not change

When your service switches to Adyen:

* the payment journey for your users will stay the same
* your team members, API keys and other service settings will stay the same
* new payments created after the switch will use Adyen

You can continue to refund payments previously processed by Stripe until the Stripe refund cut-off date. GOV.UK Pay will make this date available when it is confirmed.

## Accept Adyen as a payment provider

### What is changing

For payments processed by Adyen, the `payment_provider` field will contain:

`adyen`

instead of:

`stripe`

This affects payment information returned through:

* [Create a payment](/api_reference/create_a_payment_reference/)
* [Get information about a single payment](/api_reference/single_payment_reference/)
* [Search payments](/api_reference/search_payments_reference/)
* [webhooks](/webhooks/)

### Check your integration if

Check your integration if it:

* expects `payment_provider` to always contain `stripe`
* rejects or does not recognise other payment provider values
* uses the provider value to determine how payment information is processed

### What you need to do

Your integration must accept `adyen` as a valid value for `payment_provider`.

Do not make your integration depend on the field containing only `stripe`.

## Handle settlement information that arrives later

### What is changing

With Stripe, `settlement_summary.captured_date` becomes available almost immediately after the user confirms a payment.

With Adyen, this field may not be available immediately.

It becomes available after GOV.UK Pay receives the relevant information from Adyen. There may be a delay of several minutes.

This affects:

* [Get information about a single payment](/api_reference/single_payment_reference/)
* [Search payments](/api_reference/search_payments_reference/)

### Check your integration if

Check your integration if it expects `settlement_summary.captured_date` to be populated as soon as a payment reaches a successful or captured state.

### What you need to do

Your integration must be able to handle `settlement_summary.captured_date` being absent initially.

If you need the value, retrieve the payment again later.

The `card_payment_captured` webhook also includes this information.

## Handle payment fee information that arrives later

### What is changing

With Adyen, `net_amount` and `fee` may not be available immediately after a successful payment.

The information becomes available after GOV.UK Pay receives the relevant information from Adyen. There may be a delay of several minutes.

This can affect:

* [Get information about a single payment](/api_reference/single_payment_reference/)
* [Search payments](/api_reference/search_payments_reference/)
* [webhooks](/webhooks/)

### Check your integration if

Check your integration if it expects `net_amount` or `fee` to be populated immediately after a successful or captured payment.

### What you need to do

Your integration must be able to handle these fields being absent initially.

If you need the values soon after a payment, retrieve the payment again later.

The values will also be available when you reconcile payments using the API.

## Treat wallet card expiry dates as optional

### What is changing

For Stripe:

* `card_details.expiry_date` is always available for Apple Pay payments
* the field is not available for Google Pay payments

For Adyen, the expiry date can be available for both Apple Pay and Google Pay payments.

However, Adyen may occasionally not provide the information.

This can affect:

* [Get information about a single payment](/api_reference/single_payment_reference/)
* [Search payments](/api_reference/search_payments_reference/)
* [webhooks](/webhooks/)

### Check your integration if

Check your integration if it assumes `card_details.expiry_date` will always be present for Apple Pay payments.

### What you need to do

Treat `card_details.expiry_date` as optional for Apple Pay and Google Pay payments.

Your integration must not fail if the field is absent.

## Handle fees on Adyen refunds

### What is changing

Stripe refunds do not have a payment provider fee.

A gateway fee applies to Adyen refunds.

The `fee` and `net_amount` fields become available after GOV.UK Pay receives the relevant information from Adyen. There may be a delay of several minutes.

This can affect:

* [Check the status of a refund](/api_reference/refund_status_reference/)
* [Search refunds](/api_reference/search_refunds_reference/)
* [Get information about a payment's refunds](/api_reference/payment_refunds_reference/)

### Check your integration if

Check your integration if it:

* uses refund `fee` or `net_amount`
* assumes refunds do not have a gateway fee
* expects fee information immediately after requesting a refund

### What you need to do

Your integration must be able to handle a gateway fee on Adyen refunds.

If you need `fee` or `net_amount` shortly after requesting a refund, retrieve the refund again later.

When reconciling refunds, you can use `from_settled_date` and `to_settled_date` to retrieve refunds settled during a particular period, including the fee and net amount.

## Do not treat submitted as the final refund status

### What is changing

When you refund a Stripe payment through the API, Stripe confirms the outcome in the API response.

When you request a refund for an Adyen payment, the initial refund status returned by GOV.UK Pay is:

`submitted`

Adyen confirms the final outcome later.

### Check your integration if

Check your integration if it assumes the status returned when you request a refund is the final outcome.

### What you need to do

Do not treat `submitted` as the final refund outcome.

Accept `submitted` as the initial status.

## Include won disputes when reconciling Adyen fees

### What is changing

For Stripe disputes, a dispute fee applies only to disputes with a status of `lost`.

For Adyen disputes, a dispute fee applies to both:

* `won`
* `lost`

This affects [Search disputes](/api_reference/search_disputes_reference/).

### Check your integration if

Check your integration if it includes only `lost` disputes when calculating or reconciling dispute fees and net amounts.

### What you need to do

Include both `won` and `lost` disputes when you query and reconcile dispute fees and net amounts.

Do not assume that a dispute fee applies only to `lost` disputes.

## Check that a recurring payment agreement is active

### What is changing

With Stripe, an agreement becomes `active` immediately after the user successfully completes the payment used to set up the agreement.

With Adyen, the user will still return to your service after successfully completing the payment.

However, the agreement may not become `active` immediately.

GOV.UK Pay updates the agreement after it receives the relevant information from Adyen.

This usually happens within a few minutes.

You cannot take recurring payments until the agreement is `active`.

### Check your integration if

Check your integration if it assumes the agreement is `active` as soon as the user returns to your service after the first payment.

### What you need to do

Retrieve the agreement to check its latest status.

Treat the agreement as ready for recurring payments only when its status is `active`.

## Handle the gateway fee in comma-separated values (CSV) downloads

### What is changing

For Stripe transactions, the fee breakdown in GOV.UK Pay CSV downloads includes:

* `Fee (transaction)`
* `Fee (3DS)`
* `Fee (fraud protection)`

For Adyen transactions, it also includes:

* `Fee (gateway)`

The gateway fee also applies to refunds.

### Check your systems if

Check any integration, spreadsheet, reporting process or other system that processes fee information from GOV.UK Pay CSV downloads.

### What you need to do

Your integration, spreadsheet, reporting process or other system must be able to handle the additional `Fee (gateway)` field.

## Test changes to your integration

Plan and run tests for any changes you make to your integration.

We will update this guidance about how to test these changes when it is available.

### Check automated tests that use Stripe test cards

Adyen uses different test card details from Stripe.

Check your automated tests if they use Stripe test card details.

We will update this guidance with the Adyen test card details when they are  confirmed.

When the Adyen test cards are confirmed, update affected automated tests to use the appropriate card for each scenario.
